# Line endings are part of this repo's contract, not a display preference.
#
# Engine and bench sources are hashed into runtime and evidence contracts;
# launchers/ run under bash on the Linux boxes. A checkout that rewrites LF to
# CRLF flips every one of those digests -- so source is never EOL-converted, on
# any platform. Without this file Git for Windows applies core.autocrlf=true
# from its system gitconfig and does exactly that.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The ledger is appended to by several sessions at once, all at the same place: its end. Two
# branches that each add an entry there conflict on every rebase, and the resolution is always the
# same one -- keep both. On 2026-09-19 one pull request (#1213) met that conflict three times in
# forty minutes while main moved under it. `union` is that resolution: both sides' lines, no
# markers. Entries are named by their PR, not by a number read off the end of the file (규칙 9,
# tests/test_ledger_numbering.py), so neither their order nor their neighbour matters;
# tools/ledger_rebase.py is for the numbered sections of before and finds nothing to move now.
#
# Two things to know. It is right for an append-only file and wrong for any other: two edits to
# the SAME existing line are both kept, silently, so correct an old entry in a commit of its own
# and read the diff. And git folds the blank line both sides put before their heading into one, so
# the second entry's `###` lands directly under the first entry's last line -- it still renders
# and still parses; give it its blank line back when you see it. Git applies all this to a local
# merge or rebase; whether the pull request page still shows a conflict is the host's decision.
MEASUREMENTS.md merge=union

# Captured evidence and build outputs keep the bytes they were captured with.
measurements/** -text
*.log -text
*.gz -text
*.cubin -text
